<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Río de la Plata</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Río de la Plata is the broad estuary formed by the Paraná and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> rivers on the border of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and Uruguay, and by extension the surrounding region. Control of its eastern bank was contested between Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and Spanish, later Argentine, authorities. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> note that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a>'s ambitions there led to a costly war with Argentina over the territory that became Uruguay.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata.jpg" alt="Río de la Plata" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Sciences and Image Analysis Laboratory, NASA Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rio_de_la_Plata_BA_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…— he dissolved the Constituent Assembly in 1823 and imposed his own constitution in 1824 — and his reliance on Portuguese-born advisers alienated the Brazilian-born elite; and his ambitions in the <b>Río de la Plata</b> produced the costly Cisplatine War (1825–1828), which ended with the independence of Uruguay.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 25 August 1825 the Thirty-Three Orientals under Juan Antonio Lavalleja declared independence with the backing of the United Provinces of the <b>Río de la Plata</b>, and the 500-day Cisplatine War followed between Brazil and the United Provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Upper Peru had been transferred from the Viceroyalty of Peru to the new Viceroyalty of the <b>Río de la Plata</b> in 1776, so both Lima and Buenos Aires claimed it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The city’s legacy is visible in the palaces of the Strada Nuova and the Rolli system of lodging state guests, now a World Heritage Site, in its Baroque art, and in Genoese communities that shaped Gibraltar, Corsica, Sardinia’s Carloforte and the <b>Río de la Plata</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Paraguay grew out of the Spanish province governed from Asunción, founded in 1537, whose largely Guaraní-speaking mestizo population lived far from the centres of empire; from 1776 it belonged to the Viceroyalty of the <b>Río de la Plata</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-paraguay">Republic of Paraguay · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…(1535) and Peru (1542) were the primary administrative units, later joined by New Granada (1717, re-established 1739) and <b>Río de la Plata</b> (1776), each headed by a viceroy of high rank who exercised near-royal authority subject to the oversight of the audiencias (colonial high courts) and the Council…</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
